   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Hepatitis B is a major public health problem in Kiribati, with approximately 15% of the population living with chronic Hepatitis B infection. The Ministry of Health and Medical Services (MHMS) Hepatitis B Program introduced a &#x201c;test and treat&#x201d; strategy, but manual record keeping hampered full implementation and monitoring. In March 2024, Beyond Essential Systems assisted MHMS to implement the electronic medical record (EMR) Tamanu for the Hepatitis B Program.
&#x200d;
The Tamanu EMR enables healthcare workers to capture patient information, conduct follow-up, monitor treatment adherence, request tests and provide information to prevent mother-to-child transmission.
&#x200d;
In 2024, a total of 1,363 Hepatitis B patients in Kiribati were recorded in Tamanu as having been seen by a healthcare worker. Many of these were at a single clinic, and the data on high volumes at that clinic enabled MHMS to plan human resourcing on clinic days, including extra local nursing staff, supplemented by telehealth support from outside of Kiribati.  Program staff use geographic and demographic data from Tamanu to register positive cases into the Hepatitis B Registry enabling screening and follow-up, identifying patients who have refused treatment and those that have died. This is an essential step to reducing the burden of Hepatitis B.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Pacific island countries have prioritised the greater use of evidence to inform health policy, planning and decision making, and build resilient health systems with continuous improvement. Countries require the support of strong and enduring partnerships to make this a reality. 
&#x200d;
In 2024, through the Australian funded Pacific Health Information Support Network (PHISH) the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare (in some countries with the Pacific Community Public Health Division) completed detailed assessments of Health Information Systems in Tonga, Fji, Solomon Islands, Samoa and Vanuatu.  The assessment of health information system maturity in each country considered management and governance, policy, planning, financial resources, workforce and other factors, which then informed the development of roadmaps to guide prioritised, concrete actions to strengthen identified gaps. Some of the actions are already being delivered by PHISH (such as mentoring staff through medical terminology training), but the roadmaps can also guide country-led investment and effort for any partner working in this area.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
